    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '201. A patient with massive pulmonary embolism has RV dilation on echo, hypotension, and tachycardia. What is the first-line definitive treatment?', '["IV heparin alone", "Systemic thrombolytics (alteplase 100mg IV over 2 hours)", "Inferior vena cava filter", "Surgical embolectomy"]', 1, 'Massive PE (with hemodynamic instability) is treated with systemic thrombolytics. Alteplase 100mg IV over 2 hours is the standard regimen. Catheter-directed therapy or surgical embolectomy are alternatives.'),
    (tid, '202. In submassive PE (RV strain without hypotension), what is the role of thrombolytics?', '["Always indicated", "Controversial; consider for patients with evidence of RV dysfunction and clinical deterioration despite anticoagulation", "Never indicated", "Only for patients with positive troponin"]', 1, 'Submassive PE management is debated. Anticoagulation is standard. Thrombolytics may be considered for worsening hemodynamics, significant RV dysfunction (echo), or elevated biomarkers (troponin, BNP) indicating impending decompensation.'),
    (tid, '203. What is the Wells score used for in emergency medicine?', '["Diagnosing PE", "Estimating pre-test probability of pulmonary embolism to guide diagnostic workup", "Grading PE severity", "Predicting PE mortality"]', 1, 'The Wells score estimates clinical probability of PE (low, moderate, high). Components include clinical signs of DVT, PE most likely diagnosis, HR >100, immobilization/surgery, prior VTE, hemoptysis, and malignancy.'),
    (tid, '204. A patient with a Wells score of 1 (low probability) has a negative D-dimer. What is the next step?', '["CT pulmonary angiography", "PE is effectively ruled out; consider alternative diagnoses", "V/Q scan", "Pulmonary angiography"]', 1, 'A low pretest probability (Wells ≤4) combined with a negative high-sensitivity D-dimer effectively rules out PE with >99% negative predictive value. No further imaging is needed.'),
    (tid, '205. McConnell sign on echocardiography (RV free wall akinesis with preserved apical contractility) is suggestive of what?', '["Cardiomyopathy", "Acute right ventricular strain from pulmonary embolism", "Pericardial effusion", "Left ventricular failure"]', 1, 'McConnell sign is 77% sensitive and 94% specific for acute PE. The preserved RV apical contractility (tethered to the LV) with free wall akinesis distinguishes acute PE from chronic RV dysfunction.'),
    (tid, '206. A patient with known DVT suddenly develops dyspnea, chest pain, and oxygen desaturation. CTPA shows a saddle embolus. What is a saddle embolus?', '["A clot in a segmental artery", "A thrombus straddling the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery", "A clot in the right atrium", "A clot in the IVC"]', 1, 'A saddle embolus straddles the main pulmonary artery bifurcation, obstructing flow to both lungs. Despite its dramatic appearance, hemodynamic significance depends on the degree of obstruction and RV function.'),
    (tid, '207. Which scoring system predicts 30-day mortality in patients with confirmed pulmonary embolism?', '["Wells score", "PESI (Pulmonary Embolism Severity Index)", "CHA2DS2-VASc", "TIMI score"]', 1, 'The PESI score (and simplified sPESI) uses age, vital signs, comorbidities, and O2 saturation to stratify PE patients into risk classes for 30-day mortality, guiding inpatient vs. outpatient management.'),
    (tid, '208. A 32-year-old IV drug user presents with fever, cough, multiple bilateral peripheral lung nodules on CXR, and a tricuspid regurgitation murmur. What is the diagnosis?', '["Community-acquired pneumonia", "Right-sided endocarditis with septic pulmonary emboli", "Tuberculosis", "Lung cancer"]', 1, 'IVDU-associated right-sided endocarditis (tricuspid valve, S. aureus) causes septic emboli to the lungs, producing multiple peripheral nodular infiltrates that may cavitate. Blood cultures and echo confirm the diagnosis.'),
    (tid, '209. What are the modified Duke criteria major components for diagnosing infective endocarditis?', '["Fever and murmur", "Positive blood cultures (typical organisms in ≥2 cultures) AND endocardial involvement on echocardiography (vegetation, abscess, or new dehiscence)", "Elevated WBC and CRP", "Positive urine culture and hematuria"]', 1, 'Modified Duke criteria require 2 major, 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor criteria. Major: typical blood culture organisms AND echo findings (vegetation, abscess, prosthetic dehiscence, new regurgitation).'),
    (tid, '210. Which organism is the most common cause of acute native valve endocarditis?', '["Streptococcus viridans", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Enterococcus faecalis", "HACEK organisms"]', 1, 'S. aureus is the most common cause of acute native valve endocarditis and the most common organism in IVDU endocarditis. It has a virulent course with rapid valve destruction and high mortality.'),
    (tid, '211. Empiric antibiotic therapy for acute native valve endocarditis in the ED should cover which organism primarily?', '["Strep viridans only", "MRSA and streptococci (vancomycin + gentamicin or ceftriaxone)", "Gram-negatives only", "Fungi"]', 1, 'Empiric therapy must cover S. aureus (including MRSA) and streptococci. Vancomycin provides MRSA coverage. Gentamicin or ceftriaxone adds synergistic streptococcal and enterococcal coverage.'),
    (tid, '212. Which complication of endocarditis requires emergent surgical intervention?', '["Splinter hemorrhages", "Acute severe valvular regurgitation with heart failure refractory to medical therapy", "Janeway lesions", "Positive blood cultures after 48 hours"]', 1, 'Urgent surgery is indicated for: refractory HF from acute valvular insufficiency, uncontrolled infection despite antibiotics, large mobile vegetations >10mm with embolic events, and perivalvular abscess.'),
    (tid, '213. Roth spots, Janeway lesions, and Osler nodes are peripheral manifestations of which cardiac emergency?', '["Acute MI", "Infective endocarditis (embolic and immunologic phenomena)", "Cardiac tamponade", "Aortic dissection"]', 1, 'Roth spots (retinal hemorrhages), Janeway lesions (painless palmar/plantar macules from septic emboli), and Osler nodes (painful fingertip nodules from immune complexes) are classic endocarditis findings.'),
    (tid, '214. A patient presents with acute viral-type illness followed by chest pain, ST changes, and elevated troponin with normal coronary arteries. Cardiac MRI shows patchy myocardial enhancement. Diagnosis?', '["Acute MI", "Acute myocarditis", "Takotsubo", "Pericarditis"]', 1, 'Myocarditis presents with chest pain, arrhythmias, and heart failure after viral prodrome. Troponin is elevated. Cardiac MRI with late gadolinium enhancement (patchy, mid-wall or epicardial pattern) confirms diagnosis.'),
    (tid, '215. What is the most common cause of acute myocarditis?', '["Bacterial infection", "Viral infection (Coxsackievirus B, Parvovirus B19, adenovirus)", "Autoimmune disease", "Drug reaction"]', 1, 'Viral infections are the most common cause. Coxsackievirus B was historically dominant, but Parvovirus B19 and Human Herpesvirus 6 are now recognized as leading causes. Most cases are self-limited.'),
    (tid, '216. Giant cell myocarditis differs from typical viral myocarditis in what key way?', '["It is always benign", "It has a rapidly progressive, fulminant course with high mortality requiring immunosuppression and often transplantation", "It only affects children", "It responds to antivirals"]', 1, 'Giant cell myocarditis is a rare but devastating form with rapid hemodynamic deterioration, refractory VT, and >70% mortality without transplant. Endomyocardial biopsy shows multinucleated giant cells.'),
    (tid, '217. Blunt cardiac injury (BCI) from steering wheel impact in a motor vehicle collision is best screened with which tests?', '["Chest X-ray only", "ECG and troponin I", "CT chest with contrast", "Cardiac MRI"]', 1, 'ECG (looking for arrhythmias, ST changes, RBBB, conduction abnormalities) and troponin I are the initial screening tests for BCI. A normal ECG and normal troponin effectively exclude clinically significant BCI.'),
    (tid, '218. A trauma patient has a sternal fracture after a high-speed MVC. What cardiac injury must be ruled out?', '["Aortic dissection", "Myocardial contusion (blunt cardiac injury)", "Endocarditis", "Valvular stenosis"]', 1, 'Sternal fractures are associated with blunt cardiac injury (myocardial contusion). ECG monitoring for 24 hours is recommended. Significant BCI can cause arrhythmias, wall motion abnormalities, and rarely cardiac rupture.'),
    (tid, '219. Penetrating cardiac injury most commonly involves which chamber due to its anterior position?', '["Left atrium", "Right ventricle", "Left ventricle", "Right atrium"]', 1, 'The right ventricle is the most anterior cardiac chamber and is most commonly injured in penetrating chest trauma (stab wounds, gunshot wounds). The left ventricle is second most common.'),
    (tid, '220. A patient with a stab wound to the left chest has distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds, and hypotension. FAST exam shows pericardial fluid. What is the management?', '["Observation", "Pericardiocentesis as a bridge, followed by emergent sternotomy/thoracotomy", "CT scan", "Chest tube placement"]', 1, 'Traumatic tamponade from penetrating injury requires operative repair. Pericardiocentesis may temporize but is not definitive because the pericardial blood often clots. Emergent surgical exploration is required.'),
    (tid, '221. Traumatic aortic injury (TAI) most commonly occurs at which anatomic location?', '["Ascending aorta", "Aortic isthmus (just distal to left subclavian artery at ligamentum arteriosum)", "Descending thoracic aorta", "Abdominal aorta"]', 1, 'The aortic isthmus is the most common site of TAI due to differential deceleration forces between the mobile arch and the fixed descending aorta tethered by the ligamentum arteriosum.'),
    (tid, '222. What CXR finding is most suggestive of traumatic aortic injury?', '["Widened mediastinum >8 cm at the level of the aortic knob", "Cardiomegaly", "Bilateral effusions", "Elevated hemidiaphragm"]', 0, 'Widened mediastinum (>8 cm) is the most classic CXR finding for TAI. Other findings: loss of aortic knob contour, deviation of NG tube/trachea to the right, left apical cap, and depression of left main bronchus.'),
    (tid, '223. Cardiac concussion (commotio cordis) versus cardiac contusion: what is the key difference?', '["They are identical", "Commotio cordis is arrhythmia from impact during the vulnerable period of repolarization in a structurally normal heart; contusion is actual myocardial tissue injury", "Contusion only occurs in children", "Concussion requires surgery"]', 1, 'Commotio cordis = VF from precisely timed impact (R-on-T phenomenon) in a normal heart. Contusion = structural myocardial injury (bruising, necrosis) from blunt force, detectable by troponin elevation and wall motion abnormalities.'),
    (tid, '224. A patient with deceleration injury has acute aortic regurgitation. What mechanism is responsible?', '["Atherosclerotic plaque rupture", "Traumatic aortic root disruption or leaflet avulsion from sudden shear forces", "Rheumatic disease", "Endocarditis"]', 1, 'Deceleration injuries can cause aortic valve disruption through leaflet avulsion, annular tear, or aortic root injury. Acute severe AR causes sudden LV volume overload with pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock.'),
    (tid, '225. In a hemodynamically unstable trauma patient with suspected cardiac injury, what is the role of the FAST exam?', '["Definitive diagnosis of cardiac injury", "Rapid detection of pericardial fluid to identify tamponade requiring intervention", "Assessment of coronary anatomy", "Valve assessment"]', 1, 'The subxiphoid view of the FAST exam rapidly identifies pericardial effusion in trauma. Sensitivity is ~95% for hemopericardium. A positive finding with hemodynamic instability mandates surgical exploration.');
